@namespace SpaceSails.Client.Pages
@implements IHandleEvent
@using System.Globalization
@using SpaceSails.Client.Rendering
@using SpaceSails.Contracts
@using SpaceSails.Core
@using SpaceSails.Core.Interior
@*
    #251 item 1 · THE GALLEY CARD, ON ITS OWN FILE.

    Moved out of Map.razor by PURE MOTION — the block between the two fences below is the block that was in
    the page, character for character. Every page member it reads is a [Parameter] with the SAME NAME (the
    fields keep their underscore, the methods come across as delegates), which is what lets the markup move
    without a rewrite and lets the suite's source guards read it through MapMarkup exactly as before.

    The `@if` that opens it stays in Map.razor: the page keeps its paint order in one place, and a closed
    surface still renders nothing. IHandleEvent repeats the page's own override (Map.Sim.cs) — without it
    Blazor would put an automatic re-render back on every event in here, which the page turns off because
    the HUD's refresh belongs to OnTick's 200 ms throttle. The .razor.css beside this file carries no rules;
    it exists to give the component Map.razor.css's scope (#251 item 3 moves the rules).

    The `@{ }` around the fences is not decoration: the block used to sit inside `@if (…) { … }`,
    which is a Razor CODE block, and a code block is where its first statements are legal. The braces
    put the moved lines back in the context they were written in; they are outside the fences, so what
    the source guards read is still exactly the lines that were in the page.
*@
@* ── THE PAGE'S OWN NOTE ON THIS SURFACE, moved here with it (#251 item 1) and not touched by a
   word: it is the paragraph that stood above this block in Map.razor. It sits ABOVE the fence, not
   inside it, so that a source guard slicing the block by text goes on reading markup and not prose.
   ── *@
    @* ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       #1021 · THE GALLEY, AS A CARD.

       Owner, of the full-screen Galley desk, verbatim: "We want to keep the news feed... refactor it so it
       can be elsewhere also, but this UI MUST GO!... This was our first version, it has no gen AI or
       visibility to the bar surroundings. So keep the features but I want it done in pop-up style like the
       work the case is."

       "LIKE THE WORK THE CASE IS" IS A SPECIFICATION, and it is the card twenty lines above this one: a
       click-to-close backdrop, StopClicks so the card does not shut under the captain's own hand, an
       OverlayFrame.Bare shell whose way out is its last direct child, and the room STILL THERE behind it.
       That last clause is the owner's second complaint answered without drawing anything: opened at the
       deck's CANTINA console the card hangs over the cantina's own art, which is the "visibility to the bar
       surroundings" the blacked-out desk never had. No new picture; the room was always there, the desk was
       just standing in front of it.

       THE FEATURES, VERBATIM AND IN ORDER. Nothing on this card is new prose — the rum locker's heading,
       its tot count, its two-state steady-legs line, "Pour a tot" and its tooltip, and the wire's own words
       are all lifted from Stations/Galley.razor, which this replaces. The pour is the SAME PourRumFromGalley
       the desk called, which is the same PourRum the deck's own locker calls: one rum ledger, and now three
       doors onto it that cannot disagree about the tot count.

       THE LOCKER LEADS AND THE WIRE SCROLLS UNDER IT. On a desk the two sat side by side because there was
       a screen's width to fill; on a card there is not, so the order is the one a captain wants — the thing
       you PRESS is above the thing you READ, and the wire's list is the only part that gives (its
       CappedScrollPanel lives inside NewsWirePanel). The card's own cap is 92vh, the satchel's, so the page
       itself never scrolls. *@

@{
@* ── MARKUP BEGINS · verbatim from Map.razor ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
        <div class="view-object-backdrop" @onclick="() => Dismiss(CloseGalleyCard)">
            @* `text-light` is the desk's own, carried over: Stations/Galley.razor's root was
               `class="galley-desk text-light"`, and the tot count and the locker heading are drawn with no
               colour of their own. Dropped, they inherit the page's body grey and the card reads a shade
               dimmer than the screen it replaces — measured in a browser, not reasoned about. *@
            <OverlayShell class="galley-card text-light" StopClicks="true"
                          Frame="OverlayFrame.Bare" Dismiss="OverlayDismiss.Close"
                          DismissClass="view-object-close" DismissFace="Close"
                          DismissTitle="Shut the galley card — Esc and 6 do the same"
                          OnClose="() => Dismiss(CloseGalleyCard)">
                <div class="view-object-title">🍹 THE GALLEY</div>

                @* #1022 · THE MAN BEHIND THE COUNTER LEADS THE CARD.

                   Owner, live: "The dialog, one imagines, is a set of phrases to keep the customer talking
                   :-D ... but there is something heart warming in those scenes at the same time."

                   The picture is the gen-AI art the owner said the first version lacked, and B-7V's plate
                   and his current line sit under it, above the locker. Order matters and it is the same
                   argument #1021 made about the locker and the wire: the thing that TALKS to you is the top
                   of the card, the thing you PRESS is under it, and the thing you READ is what gives.

                   Every word here is Core's (SpaceSails.Core.TheTender) — this markup chooses nothing, it
                   only draws whichever line the sitting handed back. The rare register is drawn APART from
                   his own voice: an announcement gets its own row, indented and italic, with the line
                   underneath it in his ordinary voice. They arrive as one TheTender.Line, so there is no
                   way to render the first without the second. *@
                <div class="tender-head">
                    <img class="tender-art" src="art/b7v-the-tender.jpg" alt="" aria-hidden="true"
                         onerror="this.style.display='none'" />
                    <div class="tender-plate">@SpaceSails.Core.TheTender.Plate</div>
                    @if (_tenderLine is { } tender)
                    {
                        @if (tender.Announcement is { } announcement)
                        {
                            <div class="tender-announcement">@announcement</div>
                        }
                        <div class="tender-line">@tender.Text</div>
                    }
                </div>

                <div class="galley-card-rum">
                    <div class="fw-bold mb-2">The rum locker 🍹</div>
                    <div class="mb-1">Tots poured: @_rumTots</div>
                    <div class="mb-2 @(RumWobbleActive ? "text-warning fw-bold" : "text-secondary")">
                        @(RumWobbleActive ? "Deck's tilty — steady on. 🍹" : "Steady legs, for now.")
                    </div>
                    <button type="button" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-warning w-100 mb-2" title="Pour a tot of rum from the locker — steadies the crew's legs when the deck goes tilty" @onclick="PourRumFromGalley">
                        Pour a tot
                    </button>
                    @if (_lastRumLine is not null)
                    {
                        <div class="fst-italic text-info small">@_lastRumLine</div>
                    }
                </div>

                <SpaceSails.Client.Pages.Stations.NewsWirePanel
                    SimTime="SimTime" Feed="NewsFeed(GalleyFeedAmbientDays)"
                    ItemCount="GalleyFeedItemCount" />
            </OverlayShell>
        </div>
@* ── MARKUP ENDS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
}

@code {
    // ── WHAT THESE PARAMETERS ARE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    //
    // #251 item 1 · Every [Parameter] below carries a member of the page under THE MEMBER'S OWN NAME —
    // a field keeps its underscore, a method arrives as a delegate with its own signature, a variable the
    // page's `@if` binds arrives under the name the `@if` gave it. That is the whole trick of this
    // refactor: it is what let the markup above move out of Map.razor without a single character of it
    // changing, and what lets the suite's source guards read it through MapMarkup exactly as they read it
    // when it lived in the page. A parameter that needs saying more than that says it on its own line.

    [Parameter] public string? _lastRumLine { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public int _rumTots { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public TheTender.Line? _tenderLine { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public Action CloseGalleyCard { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public Func<Action, Task> Dismiss { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public int GalleyFeedAmbientDays { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public int GalleyFeedItemCount { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public Func<int, IReadOnlyList<NewsWire.NewsItem>> NewsFeed { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public Action PourRumFromGalley { get; set; } = default!;
    [Parameter] public bool RumWobbleActive { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public double SimTime { get; set; }

    // The page's own event dispatch, repeated: no automatic re-render per event.
    Task IHandleEvent.HandleEventAsync(EventCallbackWorkItem callback, object? arg) => callback.InvokeAsync(arg);
}
